      "page_title": "Python, Cloud, and Trade-offs",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Three Last Trade-offs",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Choice",
            "Verdict"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Static routes",
              "predictable and cheap"
            ],
            [
              "Dynamic protocols",
              "react to failure, cost CPU/RAM"
            ],
            [
              "TCAM",
              "deterministic single-cycle, pricey"
            ],
            [
              "Software trie",
              "cheap and scalable, variable latency"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Dynamic protocols also demand design discipline",
            "Providers often filter anything longer than /24 in BGP",
            "So a /27 advertisement is simply ignored on the internet"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Formula Recap",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "h = 32 - n; block size = 2^h; usable = 2^h - 2",
            "Borrow b bits and you get 2^b subnets",
            "Magic number = 256 - mask octet value",
            "Network = AND mask; broadcast = OR wildcard"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Design Order",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Count hosts per segment",
            "Round up to a power of two",
            "Allocate the largest blocks first (VLSM)",
            "Keep blocks aligned so they can aggregate later"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Next Steps",
          "text": "Convert one octet to binary daily until it is reflex, then read RFC 4632 (CIDR) and RFC 1918 (private space) end to end."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Counting Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Forgetting the -2 when counting usable hosts",
            "Subtracting 2 on a /31, where both addresses are usable",
            "Believing /25 is larger than /24",
            "Ignoring the gateway: /29 gives 6 usable, only 5 for hosts"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Assignment Mistakes",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Giving a host the network or broadcast address",
            "The interface may accept it; traffic then behaves strangely",
            "Forgetting cloud reservations (AWS 5, Azure 5) on small blocks"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Python, Cloud, and Trade-offs (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "More Trade-offs",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Choice",
            "Verdict"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Fixed-length",
              "simple to automate"
            ],
            [
              "VLSM",
              "wastes far less space"
            ],
            [
              "/30 links",
              "4 addresses, 2 usable"
            ],
            [
              "/31 links",
              "2 for 2, saves 50%"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "/30 is still the more universally supported option",
            "Few large subnets: bigger broadcast domains, noisy ARP",
            "Many small subnets: more routes, gateways, config",
            "NAT hides topology but breaks end-to-end reachability"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Aggregate Or Be Specific",
          "text": "Aggregating shrinks tables but hides failures. Deaggregating gives precise control but bloats the global table. Pick per edge, not per habit."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Alignment And Masks",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Writing 192.168.1.100/26 as a network; it is really .64/26",
            "Mask mismatch on a link: 10.0.0.5/24 next to 10.0.0.6/25",
            "ARP goes asymmetric — it appears to work one way only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "blk = 64          # /26 block size",
            "print(100 % blk)  # 36",
            "print(64 % blk)   # 0"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Plan-Level Errors",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Overlapping subnets: 10.0.0.0/23 and 10.0.1.0/24",
            "LPM still routes, but address assignment collides",
            "Overlapping RFC 1918 over a VPN -> renumber or double NAT",
            "Advertising an aggregate you do not fully own"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "172.16 Is Not The Range",
          "text": "172.16.0.0/16 is not the private Class B space. It is 172.16.0.0/12, covering 172.16 through 172.31."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Order Trap",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Routes: longest match, order-free",
            "ACLs: first match, top-down, order-critical",
            "Never debug one using the other's rule"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
